WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.380
Outrage is brewing over Mulan's new release
strategy, and it's coming from both movie

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.440
theater executives and consumers alike.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.980
Disney's live-action remake of its beloved
1998 animated film Mulan was originally meant

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:18.130
to hit theaters on March 27th, 2020, but in
response to growing concerns over the safety

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:23.080
of movie theaters amid the coronavirus crisis,
it was then delayed until July 24th.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.960
And then to August 21st.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.900
And then removed from the release schedule
altogether.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.520
On August 4th, 2020, Disney made a final,
bold decision when CEO Bob Chapek announced

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.740
that Mulan will be released directly to streaming
on Disney+ on September 4th, instead of arriving

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:39.760
in theaters.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.600
Following the announcement that one of the
most anticipated movies of 2020 will bypass

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.280
a theatrical release entirely, many have wondered
what the news could mean for the future of

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.140
movies as a whole.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.260
For some theater owners and fans, this move
by Disney is a huge problem.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.280
According to a report from Deadline, cinemas
in the UK are furious over Disney's decision

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.380
to release Mulan on streaming without even
a limited theatrical run to go alongside it.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.320
Phil Clapp, who serves as the head of the
UK Cinema Association, wrote in a statement,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.210
"The decision not to give cinemas the chance
to play the film (even if day-and-date with

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:18.680
Disney+) is frankly bewildering and something
we've of course gone back to them on."

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.080
Though Disney's Bob Chapek did describe Mulan's
release strategy as a "one-off", Clapp and

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.820
his fellow theater owners still have some
cause for concern.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.430
As the COVID-19 crisis continues throughout
the world, most places that typically attract

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.520
large crowds remain closed.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.000
While some directors, like Tenet mastermind
Christopher Nolan, have remained insistent

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.740
that their films be released theatrically,
studios are either pushing back big new releases

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.260
or removing dates from their schedules altogether.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.979
Several films, including Wes Anderson's star-studded
feature The French Dispatch, now simply have

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.300
no release date at all.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.360
Before it was officially moved to Disney+,
Mulan was facing the same fate.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.900
Many have wondered if the current crisis has
the potential to close US movie theaters for

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.400
good.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.360
Films like Tenet will skip a United States
premiere due to the fact that the outbreaks

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.010
within the States are much worse than they
are in other locations around the world, so

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:13.319
it's easy to see why UK cinema owners are
frustrated by the choice Disney made with

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:13.960
Mulan.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.080
Still, they may have to adjust to a rapidly
shifting cinematic landscape.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.140
Unfortunately for Disney, the company didn't
just tick off UK cinema owners; the Mouse

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.420
House also irritated audiences.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.360
However, fans aren't upset that they can't
see Mulan in theaters and instead have to

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.820
watch the flick from the comfort of their
own homes; they're angry at the price of entry.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.660
When word broke about Mulan heading to Disney+,
the news came with a hefty catch: viewers

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:44.120
will have to pay $29.99 to watch the movie...in
addition to their usual subscription costs,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.240
far above the price for a single movie ticket.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.120
However once purchased, subscribers can watch
Mulan as many times as they please, unlike

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.640
in theaters which requires a fee for every
showing.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.120
That doesn't mean Disney won't have a hard
time getting viewers to pony up that kind

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.360
of money, especially when they already pay
for a Disney+ subscription.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.020
Adding insult to injury for some is the fact
that the live-action Mulan differs too much

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.200
from the animated original, with one Twitter
user writing.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.540
"So they're making us pay for it while paying
for the subscription?

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.280
For a movie that has no music and no Mushu?

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.980
Nah fam, I'm good."

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.340
Another added,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.780
"I wouldn't pay $30 for a movie ticket in
the theater.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.520
So I'm not going to pay that to see the new
Mulan, especially when it doesn't have Mushu

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.880
or classic songs [from] the animated version."

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.599
On the flip side, others have noted that $30
is a small price to pay to watch a blockbuster

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:36.599
movie like Mulan with your entire family,
and that it's important to support a movie

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.110
led by an all-Asian cast.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.600
"You know, you just gotta…gotta learn to
let these things go."

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.879
Overall, while many are unhappy with the decision
Disney made, only time will tell how things

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:52.069
pan out in terms of both critical response
to the film and the movie's financial gain

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:53.400
from this type of debut.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.220
Those interested can rent Mulan on Disney+
beginning September 4th, 2020.

